<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 伊凡塞斯的《我的不朽》]

00:48:43.090 --> 00:49:11.370
<v Ira Glass>Catalina Puente in New York with WNYC's Radio Rookies, whose story was produced by Serena Patel with assistance from Miguel Macias, Wayne Schulmister, and Karen Michel. Other stories by Radio Rookies at www.radiorookies.org. Catalina's story was supported by Con Edison, the Fred L. Emerson Foundation, the National Endowment for the Arts, Open Society Institute's Youth Initiatives program, the Helena Rubinstein Foundation, the Surdna Foundation, Rockefeller Philanthropy Advisors, the New York Times Company Foundation, and Time Warner.
<v Ira Glass>卡塔琳娜·普恩特在纽约参加WNYC的“广播新秀”节目，该故事由塞雷娜·帕特尔制作，并得到米格尔·马西亚斯、韦恩·舒尔米斯特和凯伦·米歇尔的协助。更多“广播新秀”故事请访问 www.radiorookies.org。卡塔琳娜的故事得到了爱迪生联合公司、弗雷德·L·爱默生基金会、国家艺术基金会、开放社会研究所青年倡议项目、海伦娜·鲁宾斯坦基金会、瑟德纳基金会、洛克菲勒慈善咨询公司、《纽约时报》公司基金会和时代华纳的支持。

00:49:11.370 --> 00:49:20.180
<v Ira Glass>Act Three. So a Squirrel and a Chipmunk Walk Into a Bar. We end our program today with this fable of doomed love from David Sedaris.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，花栗鼠和松鼠走进一家酒吧。我们以戴维·塞达里斯的这个注定失败的爱情寓言结束今天的节目。

00:49:20.180 --> 00:49:49.170
<v David Sedaris>The squirrel and the chipmunk had been dating for two weeks when they ran out of things to talk about. Acorns, parasites, the inevitable approach of autumn, these subjects had been covered within their first hour. And so, breathlessly, their faces had flushed. Twice they had held long conversations about dogs, each declaring their across-the-board hatred of them, and speculating on what life might be like were someone to put a bowl of food in front of them two times a day.
<v David Sedaris>松鼠和花栗鼠约会两周后，就没话可说了。橡子、寄生虫、秋天不可避免的临近，这些话题在他们第一个小时里就聊完了。于是，他们面红耳赤，气喘吁吁。他们曾两次长谈关于狗的话题，每人都宣称对狗完全讨厌，并猜测如果有人每天给它们放两碗食物，生活会是什么样子。

00:49:49.170 --> 00:49:53.390
<v David Sedaris>"They're spoiled rotten, is what it comes down to," the chipmunk had said.
<v David Sedaris>“它们被宠坏了，归根结底就是这样，”花栗鼠曾说。

00:49:53.390 --> 00:50:02.000
<v David Sedaris>And the squirrel had placed his paw over hers, saying, "That's it, exactly. Finally, someone who really gets it."
<v David Sedaris>松鼠把爪子放在她的爪子上，说：“就是这样，一点没错。终于有人真正理解了。”

00:50:02.000 --> 00:50:30.320
<v David Sedaris>Friends had warned them that their romance could not possibly work out. And such moments convinced them that these naysayers were not just wrong, but jealous. "They'll never have what we do," the squirrel would say. And then the two of them would sit quietly, hoping for a flash flood or a rifle report, something, anything that might generate a conversation.
<v David Sedaris>朋友们警告他们这段恋情不可能成功。而那一刻让他们确信这些反对者不仅错了，而且是嫉妒。“他们永远不会拥有我们这样的一切，”松鼠会说。然后两人会静静地坐着，希望能有山洪暴发或枪声，什么都行，任何能引发对话的事情。

00:50:30.320 --> 00:50:44.600
<v David Sedaris>They were out one night at a little bar run by a couple of owls when, following a long silence, the squirrel slapped his palm against the table top. "You know what I like?" he said. "I like jazz."
<v David Sedaris>一天晚上他们在一对猫头鹰经营的小酒吧里，经过长时间的沉默，松鼠猛地拍了一下桌子。“你知道我喜欢什么吗？”他说。“我喜欢爵士乐。”

00:50:44.600 --> 00:51:03.470
<v David Sedaris>"I didn't know that," the chipmunk said. "My goodness. Jazz." She had no idea what jazz was, but worried that asking would make her sound stupid and unworthy of his affections. "What kind, exactly?" she asked, hoping his answer might narrow things down a bit.
<v David Sedaris>“我不知道，”花栗鼠说。“天哪。爵士乐。”她根本不知道什么是爵士乐，但担心问出来会显得自己很蠢，配不上他的深情。“到底是什么样的？”她问，希望他的回答能缩小一下范围。

00:51:03.470 --> 00:51:09.020
<v David Sedaris>"Well, all kinds really," he told her, "especially the earlier stuff."
<v David Sedaris>“嗯，各种各样的都有，真的，”他告诉她，“尤其是早期的东西。”

00:51:09.020 --> 00:51:28.450
<v David Sedaris>"Me too," she said. And when he asked her why, she told him that the later stuff was just a little too late for her tastes, almost like it was overripe or something, you know what I mean? And for the third time since she had known him, the squirrel reached across the table and took her paw.
<v David Sedaris>“我也是，”她说。当他问为什么时，她告诉他后期的东西对她的口味来说太晚了，就像是熟过头了一样，你知道我的意思吗？这是她认识他以来第三次，松鼠伸过手抓住了她的爪子。

00:51:28.450 --> 00:51:39.880
<v David Sedaris>On returning home that evening, the chipmunk woke her older sister, with whom she shared a room. "Listen," she whispered. "I need you to explain something. What's jazz?"
<v David Sedaris>那天晚上回到家，花栗鼠叫醒了她同住的姐姐。“听着，”她低声说。“我需要你解释一下。什么是爵士乐？”

00:51:39.880 --> 00:51:42.970
<v David Sedaris>"Why are you asking me?" the sister said.
<v David Sedaris>“你为什么问我？”姐姐说。

00:51:42.970 --> 00:51:45.590
<v David Sedaris>"So you don't know either?" the chipmunk asked.
<v David Sedaris>“所以你也不知道？”花栗鼠问。

00:51:45.590 --> 00:51:53.310
<v David Sedaris>"I didn't say I didn't know," the sister said. "I asked you why you're asking. Does this have anything to do with that squirrel?"
<v David Sedaris>“我没说我不知道，”姐姐说。“我问你为什么要问。这跟那只松鼠有关系吗？”

00:51:53.310 --> 00:51:55.760
<v David Sedaris>"Maybe," the chipmunk said.
<v David Sedaris>“也许吧，”花栗鼠说。

00:51:55.760 --> 00:52:23.390
<v David Sedaris>"Well, I'm telling," the sister announced. "First thing tomorrow morning, because this has gone on long enough." She punched at her pillow of moss, then repositioned it beneath their head. "I warned you weeks ago that this wouldn't work out. And now you've got the whole house in an uproar, waltzing home in the middle of the night, waking me up with your dirty little secrets. Jazz indeed. You just wait until Mother hears about this."
<v David Sedaris>“好啊，我去告状，”姐姐宣布。“明天一早，因为这事儿够久了。”她捶了捶苔藓枕头，重新在她们的头下放好。“几周前我就警告过你，这不会有好结果的。现在你把家里闹得鸡犬不宁，大半夜回家，用你那些肮脏的小秘密吵醒我。爵士乐，哼。你等着听妈怎么说吧。”

00:52:23.390 --> 00:52:59.400
<v David Sedaris>The chipmunk lay awake that night, imagining the unpleasantness that was bound to take place the following morning. What if jazz was squirrel slang for something terrible, like anal intercourse? "Oh, I like it too," she'd said, and so eagerly. Then again, it could just be mildly terrible, something along the lines of Communism or fortune-telling, subjects that were talked about but hardly ever practiced. Just as she thought she'd calmed herself down, a new possibility would enter her mind, each one more terrible than the last.
<v David Sedaris>花栗鼠那天晚上躺着睡不着，想象着第二天早上不可避免要发生的不愉快。如果“爵士乐”是松鼠关于某种可怕事情的俚语，比如肛交呢？“哦，我也喜欢，”她刚才说得那么热切。再说，它也可能只是稍微可怕一点，类似于共产主义或算命，这些话题常被讨论但几乎从不实践。就在她以为自己平静下来时，一个新的可能性涌上心头，每一个都比上一个更可怕。

00:52:59.400 --> 00:53:17.050
<v David Sedaris>Jazz was a maggot-infested flesh of a dead body. The ochre crust on an infected eye. Another word for ritual suicide. And she had claimed to like it.
<v David Sedaris>爵士乐是死尸身上长满蛆虫的肉。感染眼睛上的赭色结痂。或者是仪式性自杀的另一个词。而她竟然说她喜欢。

00:53:17.050 --> 00:53:39.720
<v David Sedaris>Years later, when she could put it all in perspective, she'd realized that she'd never really trusted the squirrel. How else to explain all those terrible possibilities? Had he been another chipmunk, even a tough one, she'd have assumed that jazz was something familiar, a kind of root, say, or maybe a hairstyle. Of course, her sister hadn't helped any. None of her family had.
<v David Sedaris>多年后，当她能从全局看问题时，她意识到她从未真正信任过那只松鼠。否则怎么解释所有那些可怕的可能性？如果他是另一只花栗鼠，哪怕是强壮的那种，她也会认为爵士乐是某种熟悉的东西，比如一种根茎，或者是某种发型。当然，她姐姐没帮上忙。她的家人都没帮上忙。

00:53:39.720 --> 00:53:58.870
<v David Sedaris>"It's not that I have anything against squirrels, per se," her mother had said. "It's just that this one-- well, I don't like him." When pressed for details, she'd mentioned his fingernails, which were a little too long for her tastes. "A sure sign of vanity," she warned. "And then there's this jazz business."
<v David Sedaris>“并不是我本质上对松鼠有什么偏见，”她母亲曾说。“只是这一只——嗯，我不喜欢他。”当被追问细节时，她提到了他的指甲，那对她的口味来说太长了。“这是虚荣的铁证，”她警告道。“还有这该死的爵士乐。”

00:53:58.870 --> 00:54:06.220
<v David Sedaris>That was what did it. Following a sleepless night, the chipmunk's mother had forced her to break it off.
<v David Sedaris>这就是原因。经过一个不眠之夜，花栗鼠的母亲强迫她结束了这段感情。

00:54:06.220 --> 00:54:11.390
<v David Sedaris>"Well, the squirrel had sighed. "I guess that's that."
<v David Sedaris>“嗯，”松鼠叹了口气。“我想就这样吧。”

00:54:11.390 --> 00:54:21.130
<v David Sedaris>"I guess it is," the chipmunk said. He headed downriver a few days later, and she never saw him or spoke to him again.
<v David Sedaris>“我想也是，”花栗鼠说。几天后他顺流而下，她再也没见过他，也没和他说过话。

00:54:21.130 --> 00:54:28.330
<v David Sedaris>"It's no great loss," her sister said. "No girl should be subjected to language like that, especially from the likes of him."
<v David Sedaris>“没什么损失，”姐姐说。“任何女孩都不应该受到那种语言的折磨，尤其是像他那样的人。”

00:54:28.330 --> 00:54:36.710
<v David Sedaris>"Amen," her mother added.
<v David Sedaris>“阿门，”母亲补充道。

00:54:36.710 --> 00:54:57.160
<v David Sedaris>Eventually, the chipmunk met someone else, and after she had safely married, her mother speculated that perhaps Jazz was a branch of medicine, something like chiropractic therapy that wasn't quite legitimate. Her sister said, no. It was more likely a jig. And then she pushed herself back from the table and kicked her chubby legs into the air.
<v David Sedaris>最终，花栗鼠遇到了别人，在她安稳地结婚后，她母亲猜测也许爵士乐是医学的一个分支，类似于脊椎按摩疗法，不太正规。姐姐说，不是。更有可能是跳吉格舞。然后她把身子往后一仰，把圆滚滚的腿踢向空中。

00:54:57.160 --> 00:55:13.250
<v David Sedaris>"Oh, you," her mother said. "That's a can-can." And then she joined in and gave a few kicks of her own. This stuck in the squirrel's mind, for she never knew her mother could identify a dance step, or anything associated with fun.
<v David Sedaris>“哦，你呀，”母亲说。“那是康康舞。”然后她也加入进来，自己踢了几下。这印在松鼠的脑海里，因为她从不知道她母亲能辨认出舞步，或是任何与乐趣相关的东西。

00:55:13.250 --> 00:55:48.740
<v David Sedaris>It was the way her own children would eventually think of her: dull, strict, chained to the past. She had boys, all of them healthy and only one prone to trouble. He had a habit of being in the wrong place at the wrong time, but his heart was good, and the chipmunk knew he would eventually straighten himself out. Her husband thought so too, and died knowing that he had been correct. A month or two after he'd passed on, she asked this son what Jazz was, and when he told her it was a kind of music, she knew immediately that he was telling the truth.
<v David Sedaris>这就是她自己的孩子最终对她的看法：沉闷、严格、束缚于过去。她生了男孩，都很健康，只有一个容易惹麻烦。他习惯于在错误的时间出现在错误的地点，但他心地善良，花栗鼠知道他最终会变好的。她的丈夫也是这么认为的，死时就知道他是对的。他去世一两个月后，她问这个儿子什么是爵士乐，当他告诉她那是一种音乐时，她立刻知道他说的是实话。

00:55:48.740 --> 00:55:51.830
<v David Sedaris>"Is it bad music?" she asked.
<v David Sedaris>“那是坏音乐吗？”她问。

00:55:51.830 --> 00:55:57.480
<v David Sedaris>"Well, if it's played badly," he said. "Otherwise, it's really quite pleasant."
<v David Sedaris>“嗯，如果演奏得很烂的话，”他说。“否则，它实际上非常悦耳。”

00:55:57.480 --> 00:55:59.490
<v David Sedaris>"Did squirrels invent it?"
<v David Sedaris>“是松鼠发明的吗？”

00:55:59.490 --> 00:56:03.390
<v David Sedaris>"God, no," he said. "Whoever gave you that idea?"
<v David Sedaris>“天哪，不，”他说。“谁给你这种想法的？”

00:56:03.390 --> 00:56:20.240
<v David Sedaris>The chipmunk stroked her brown and white muzzle. "Nobody," she said. "I was just guessing."
<v David Sedaris>花栗鼠抚摸着她棕白相间的口鼻部。“没谁，”她说。“我只是在瞎猜。”

00:56:20.240 --> 00:56:57.670
<v David Sedaris>When her muzzle grew more white than brown, the chipmunk forgot that she and the squirrel had had nothing to talk about. She forgot the definition of Jazz as well, and came to think of it as every beautiful thing she had ever failed to appreciate: the taste of warm rain, the smell of a baby, the din of a swollen river rushing past her tree and onward to infinity.
<v David Sedaris>当她的口鼻部变得白多棕少时，花栗鼠忘记了她和那只松鼠曾没什么可聊的。她也忘记了爵士乐的定义，并把它看作是她一生中未能欣赏的所有美好事物：温暖雨水的味道、婴儿的气味、湍急河流掠过她树木奔向无限远方的喧嚣。

00:56:57.670 --> 00:57:27.930
<v Ira Glass>David Sedaris' most recent book is Dress Your Family in Corduroy and Denim. He's also the editor of an anthology of favorite short stories called Children Playing Before a Statue of Hercules .
<v Ira Glass>戴维·塞达里斯最近的书是《穿灯芯绒和牛仔裤的家人》。他也是短篇小说选集《在赫拉克勒斯雕像前玩耍的孩子》的编辑。

00:57:27.930 --> 00:57:42.240
<v Ira Glass>Our program was produced today by Jane Feltes and myself, with Alex Blumberg, Diane Cook, Sarah Koenig, Amy O'Leary, and Lisa Pollak. Our senior producer is Julie Snyder. Elizabeth Meister runs our website. Production help from Sam Hallgren, Thea Chaloner, Seth Lind, and Tommy Andres. Music help from Jessica Hopper.
<v Ira Glass>今天的节目由简·费尔特斯和我制作，参与者还有亚历克斯·布隆伯格、黛安·库克、莎拉·柯尼格、艾米·奥利里和丽莎·波拉克。我们的资深制作人是朱莉·斯奈德。伊丽莎白·迈斯特负责我们的网站。萨姆·哈尔格伦、西娅·查洛纳、塞思·林德和汤米·安德烈提供了制作帮助。杰西卡·霍珀提供了音乐帮助。

00:57:42.240 --> 00:57:51.350
